Given below are two statements:
<table border="1" cellpadding="1" cellspacing="1" height="103" width="573"><tbody><tr><td><strong>Statement -I</strong></td><td>The contact angle between a solid and a liquid is a property of the material of the solid and liquid as well.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Statement -II</strong></td><td>The rise of a liquid in a capillary tube does not depend on the inner radius of the tube.</td></tr></tbody></table>
&nbsp;

<table border="1" cellpadding="1" cellspacing="1" height="78" width="498"><tbody><tr><td>1.</td><td><strong>Statement I </strong>is false but <strong>Statement II</strong> is true.</td></tr><tr><td>2.</td><td><div>Both <strong>Statement I</strong> and <strong>Statement II</strong> are false.</div></td></tr><tr><td>3.</td><td><div>Both <strong>Statement I</strong> and <strong>Statement II</strong> are true.</div></td></tr><tr><td>4.</td><td><div><strong>Statement I</strong> is true but <strong>Statement II</strong> is false.</div></td></tr></tbody></table>&nbsp;

questions.explanation:
Hint:\(P=P_0+h\rho g\)

Step: Find the value of\(h\).
The change in pressure due to height is given by;
\(\Delta P = \rho g h\)

The change in pressure due to the rotation of the cylindrical vessel is given
by;
\(\Delta P = \frac{\rho \omega^2 r^2}{2}\)
The pressure at the point\(C\)along\(BC\)is given by;
\(P_{C}= P_0 + \rho gh~~~...(1)\)
The pressure at the point\(C\)along\(AC\)is given by;
\(P_{C}= P_0 +\frac{\rho \omega^2 r^2}{2}~~~...(2)\)
From the equations \((1)\) and \((2)\) we get;
\(P_0 + \rho gh= P_0 +\frac{\rho \omega^2 r^2}{2}\)
\(\Rightarrow h = \frac{\omega^2 r^2}{2g}\)
\(\Rightarrow h = \frac{25\omega^2}{2g}\)
